~ start


Pili: Ernesto, look at the water level outside! It's already up to the porch steps! We need to grab our bags and leave NOW.
Ernesto: Leave? Pili, are you out of your mind? Look at those winds out there. If we step outside now, we’ll get swept right down the street.
Pili: The flood warning went off two hours ago! I told you we should have evacuated early, but you kept saying the storm would turn!
Ernesto: Because the house is solid! It’s held up through every typhoon for the last twenty years. The safest place for us is right here, inside these walls.
Pili: Solid? Look at the floorboards! The water is seeping in! If we wait any longer, the road will be completely impassable. We have to go to the shelter!
Ernesto: It's too late for the shelter, Pili! Driving or walking in this current is suicide. We are staying in the house.

A loud crash is heard outside as a large tree branch hits the side of the house.

Pili: Did you hear that?! The water is rising faster now! We can't stay on the ground floor anymore.
Ernesto: Fine! Fine, you’re right about the ground floor, but we are NOT going outside into that torrent. We need an escape route inside.

Pili: The roof hatch in the attic! We can climb up to the attic and get onto the roof so the rescue boats can spot us!
Ernesto: The attic? Pili, that’s a death trap! If the water keeps rising and fills the second floor, we’ll be trapped under the rafters with nowhere to go!
Pili: But the roof hatch gets us outside into the open!
Ernesto: Not if we get pinned inside before we can open it! The master bedroom window on the second floor makes way more sense.
Pili: The window? Ernesto, the wind is blowing straight against that side of the house! If we open that window, the storm will tear the room apart!
Ernesto: But it’s an immediate exit to the porch roof! We don't risk getting cornered in a dark attic!

Water surges against the front door with a heavy thud. Time is running out.


=> END